body:
The module system [[Node.js]] shipped with, back when JavaScript had no built-in one. Server-side code needed [[Encapsulation]] — a way for code in one package not to interfere with another's — and CommonJS was the answer.

Two halves:
```js
// exporting, in maths.js
module.exports = { add, subtract };

// importing, elsewhere
const maths = require("./maths");
const http = require("http");   // built-in module
```

`require()` returns *something*, typically an object, and that something is whatever the required file assigned to `module.exports`. Built-in modules (`http`, `fs`, `path`) come with Node; everything else must be installed first.

The important contrast is with ES modules (`import`/`export`), which are the standard today and are what browsers understand natively. `require` is resolved at runtime and can be called conditionally, whereas `import` is static and analysable at build time — which is precisely why a [[Module Bundler]] can do [[Tree Shaking]] on ES modules but generally cannot on CommonJS. See [[Module]] and [[Package]].
